Forget the idea that the universe is made of "things" floating in an empty room.
There are no pre-existing objects, no background grids, and no canvas. At the absolute bedrock, there is only The Unbreakable Law: the ironclad requirement that logical relations must balance, and contradictions must instantly erase themselves.
You don't need a rule-maker or a guard to enforce it. A broken equation simply cannot exist; it self-cancels into nothingness. Only consistency survives, and survival builds a history.
This repository explores how that raw, self-enforcing constraint bootstraps itself upward—how intersections stabilize, how structures refuse to clump into knots, and how three-dimensional space emerges simply out of logic keeping its own books.

---

### How It Works

Forget the idea that you can just let random data mix together and expect a universe to pop out. If you let a network run wild without supervision, it doesn't build a world—it collapses into a dense, tangled knot of garbage that goes nowhere.

To keep the system from getting lazy and folding in on itself, the framework relies on a few simple rules:

* **The Balance Sheet:** There are no floating numbers or abstract variables here. There is only a relentless accounting of relationships. Every connection has to balance its books. If a transaction doesn't close cleanly, it doesn't get filed away—it gets thrown out.
* **The Personal Space Rule:** Without guardrails, everything in a network wants to pile onto the exact same spot. To force the system to actually *grow*, the framework enforces a strict "no-stacking" rule. Pieces aren't allowed to pile on top of each other like cheap bricks. They have to push apart, spread out, and give each other room to breathe.

### The Result

Because the ledger refuses to accept broken math, and because the pieces are forced to keep their distance, something incredible happens. The tangled ball of string opens up. It stabilizes.

You didn't program a three-dimensional universe into the code. You didn't hardcode height, width, and depth. You just set up the rules of the ledger, stepped back, and watched space build itself out of nothing.
